ies are  so young and they're so far away from  going public but i think the pullback in  a lot of the large money managers that  were  pumping up the valuations for startups  last year the fact that they're no  longer in the market  means that a lot of these earlier  companies can't really raise follow-on  rounds of funding  and  venture capitals that are backing these  younger companies relied on those big  grounds and those large markups to  generate a paper return on their funds  and so now i think early stage vcs are  understandably nervous about backing  companies that might not be able to  generate  added funding in future financing rounds  and so i think the broader slowdown in  the  market for more mature startups is sort  of  seeping into the early stage  investing environment and i think  generally the sort of nervousness around  you know how severe the cooldown in the  vc market will be is is just causing  investors across the spectrum to become  very careful  in selecting startups to back  so how are early stage startups handling  this  some some startups are in a tough  position because they raised a lot of  money at high prices last year before  they really developed  products that were proven to have  traction in the market and be popular  with users so a lot of those companies i  mean they're running out of cash they  don't have stable revenue streams and  what's happening is that they're turning  to their existing investors and asking  for these  emergency funding rounds essentially  trying to get you know a few million  more dollars on their balance sheet at  the same price as their previous round  to try and ride out you know the sort of  tougher fundraising environment but it  definitely puts even these younger  companies in a tough spot because  it'll be hard for them to raise cash at  higher prices than what they raised last  year  given how much more discerning investors  are being with  you know evaluating financial metrics  and making sure 